nginx-quic-exiting-worker.patch  (applies to nginx 1.31.8 as bundled in OpenResty 1.31.8.1)

HTTP/3 after an nginx reload, without quic_bpf.

Problem (reproduced on 2026-11-11 with this image, 30 ms round trip): after `nginx -s reload`
the retired workers keep the QUIC listening sockets open, and the new worker with the same
number reads the same reuseport UDP socket.  Whichever process calls recvmsg() first gets the
datagram, so a retired worker takes away datagrams of the new worker's connections.  A retired
worker lives as long as any of its connections does (a websocket over TCP is enough), up to
worker_shutdown_timeout (120 s here).  Measured during that window: 4 of 40 new HTTP/3
requests failed, 16 of 40 needed more than 0.5 s, uploads failed.

What the patch does, in a worker that is shutting down ONLY (ngx_exiting):
  1. ngx_quic_recvmsg(): if none of the worker's QUIC connections uses the listening socket
     any more, the worker gives it up (event deleted, descriptor closed in this process) and
     leaves the datagram that woke it in the queue for the new worker.
  2. NGX_QUIC_EXITING_GRACE (3 s) after the first datagram the worker sees while shutting
     down, its remaining QUIC connections are closed (ngx_close_idle_connections() ->
     ngx_quic_input_handler(), which now closes instead of waiting for the streams once the
     grace period is over), so the socket is given up shortly after.  Clients retry such
     HTTP/3 requests on a new connection.  HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2 connections are not touched
     and keep the full worker_shutdown_timeout.
  3. Until then a datagram of a connection the worker does not know is dropped; it used to
     create a connection object and answer with a stateless reset.

Measured with the patch (same test): with 16 websockets keeping all four retired workers
alive, 40 of 40 new HTTP/3 requests answered at normal speed and uploads ran at full speed
during the window.  With HTTP/3, HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 transfers in flight at the reload, the
HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 transfers completed, the HTTP/3 ones were closed after the grace period,
and new HTTP/3 requests kept working.  12 reloads in 36 s under mixed load: no worker crash,
no descriptor leak, no failed HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 request.

Nothing changes for a worker that is not shutting down.  quic_bpf builds are unaffected (the
listening sockets are closed by ngx_close_listening_sockets() there).

diff -ruN a/src/event/quic/ngx_event_quic.c b/src/event/quic/ngx_event_quic.c
